    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d32">

                <pb facs="0110" n="20" sameAs="frus:frus1918Russiav02/pg20"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 861.00/916</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Morris</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1918-01-07T20:00:00+09:00"><hi rendition="#italic">January 7,
                                1918, 8 p.m.</hi></date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received January 7, 4.50
                        p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Upon my communicating with Vice Minister for Foreign Affairs, your telegram of
                    January 5, 3 p.m.,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d32-f1"/> he advised me that 26th Lord
                        Robert Cecil had urged upon Japanese
                    Ambassador in London the inadvisableness of antagonizing Maximalist Party now in
                    control in Russia, but that on the 1st instant he had urged the necessity of
                    taking some steps short of actual military intervention to protect military
                    stores lying at Vladivostok. Vice Minister for Foreign Affairs said his
                    Government is waiting further explanation of the apparent change of attitude of
                    the British Government and of its immediate intentions before taking final
                    decision. In reply to inquiry as to his personal feeling, he stated he thought
                    it would be premature to land troops, as he understood British Foreign Office
                    proposes, inasmuch as that might incur anti-Ally feeling.</p>
                <p>Stevens announces credit entirely
                    satisfactorily arranged.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Morris</hi></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
